On Sunday, May 28, 2017 at 10:01:20 AM UTC-4, Bob Vines wrote:
>
>
>
> On Friday, May 26, 2017 at 5:07:49 AM UTC-4, clasystems wrote:
>>
>> There are some issues that must be resolved before the PEPS for Windows 
>> package can be released fully PD.  I would like to give details and get 
>> some feedback as to best solve them without undue holding up of the release.
>>
>>
>> One of the capabilities of PEPS is to create PDF files out of such as the 
>> output of PDP-8 assemblers such as P?S/8 PAL or OS/8 PAL8.  Towards that 
>> end, there is a neat package known as the Da Vince [yeah, he has a pun in 
>> there] Tools.
>>
>> There are a whole class of programs that evolved over the years to a 
>> divergent set of third-party applications that are used in quite 
>> incompatible ways for this purpose.
>>
>> Within the PEPS package, the usual output file is called LPTOUT.TXT.  
>> This is created whenever device 66 LPT output is created within a running 
>> SIMH session.
>>
>>
>> In general, there is a surprising amount of problems with these files due 
>> to various artifacts and other unexpected collections of junk, some of 
>> which is unfathomable, yet it is definitely present.  [For example, the 
>> particulars of junk change in PAL8 as a function of exact command-;line 
>> options that just doesn't make any sense, yet can be easily demonstrated.]
>>
>> This collection of problems is totally solved by a component of the 
>> Package that applies a "hairy" TECO macro I wrote that requires Pete 
>> Siemsen's TECOC for Windows; there are unique features in this version of 
>> TECO that are vital when dealing with the incompatible conventions of PDP-8 
>> programs versus the byte counts of DOS and Windows, etc.  To within a minor 
>> bug, STRIP.TEC handles all cases of known junk to clean up.  [Note: I have 
>> documentation on how the macro works that will be part of the package.  It 
>> helped me find some former bugs, but this one eludes me.  The issue is that 
>> if you use the macro to clean up output from the PTP: which adds in 
>> additional junk which I can explain exactly what and the bad decisions as 
>> to why.  The file will be left with an extraneous <FF> character at the end 
>> occasionally.  However, if you run the file through the macro a second 
>> time, it will be removed.  This never happens with the LPT: output stream.  
>> While this is not completely understood, the circumstances as to why this 
>> is even present are far more complicated than you might expect.]  
>> Regardless, any output file can be made ready for the next step.
>>
>> To use the Da Vince tools is a for less cumbersome way, the command-line 
>> tool for converting text files to .PDF is used, as is the tradition for 
>> this class of utilities.  In this particular case, you have to have a 
>> specific .ini file in the same directory, which separates this one from 
>> most of the others, which take purely command-line augmentations, etc.
>>
>> The file is a bunch of scenarios that cover a lot of irrelevant modes of 
>> Da Vince's package, and I have made some custom changes to the relevant 
>> section so it suits our needs, which is not quite what it is using the 
>> default values.
>>
>> However, the changes are fairly trivial.
>>
>> So, the first issue:
>>
>> Am I legally allowed to distribute this ASCII text file which is 99.9 % a 
>> small part of their package and .1 % my own minor work?  And if that is not 
>> the case, I then have to document what to change, perhaps in some form such 
>> as the output of the typical DOS FC command [perhaps embellished with stuff 
>> like reference some unique line and then say the changes come x lines or so 
>> after that?  To my knowledge, no copyright is expressly specific to the 
>> file itself, but the more I have to do, the more it means users have to 
>> tweak PEPS and the more I have to write up in PEPS documentation, etc.
>>
>>
> Perhaps you could provide your modified .ini file with a different name 
> for users to use to replace the "official" .ini file?
>

I am not sure just how much of this file can be bodily removed.  Most of it 
seems to address specific sub-modes, and I only use one of sub-modes. 
 Thus, there are two general methods of attack:

1) Create an editing script of what to add/remove, then save the entire 
file.

I can also provide the output of the MS-DOS FC program because it is that 
trivial.

It is also conceivable I can work up a BATCH file that invokes the SED 
editor;  From what I gather, it was created for stuff just like this. 
 Worst case, I can come up with a TECOC macro to do it also.  [I sorta like 
the latter more than the former, and it is probably better for me to do 
some familiar work than to also learn how to do some unfamiliar work, no 
matter how trivial.  The effort to create STRIP.TEC to work with TECOC is 
much more than is necessary to accomplish this relatively small change.]

2) Attempt to write my own .ini file that is the bare-bones minimum that 
works.  I think I can then claim copyright on that .ini file as long as I 
reference it is for use with Da Vince which I make clear I have no 
connection with but recommend it.

Clearly the former is just providing instructions to end users.  The latter 
might not legally pass muster as easily.

In any case, you have to "lift" the binary out of the installation and 
place it into a special directory within the PEPS package, so I cannot 
escape that aspect of things.  BTW, this applies whether you register the 
program or not.  I have no idea what personal information winds up in the 
registered copy; draw your own conclusion about what might constitute usage 
in violation of the rights bestowed upon anyone by purchase.  Also, the 
cost of this package is $99 which is pretty steep considering that only a 
fraction of the capability is being used.  If I were the owner and wanted 
$99 for everything, I would accept $10 for just this part of it, but I'm 
not Da Vince.

In any case, this is likely a factor on why despite the quite obvious bugs 
it has overall, no actual changes have apparently been made since around 
2005.

 

>
>  
>
>> I think I am on pretty clear ground with the related issue:
>>
>> You have to install Da Vince Tools the default way and you will find 
>> within it the executable [and the .ini file as mentioned above] in a 
>> subdirectory I can document.  You have to copy that binary file into a 
>> specific directory within the PEPS package.
>>
>> The .ini file too?
>

Answered that above, assuming you edit it instead of I write another one 
which as stated above might still be a problem. 

>
>  
>
>> I see no legal way to include this file, so I am going to have to 
>> document how to do this at least.
>>
>> There is a third issue which gets to more serious consequences.  The 
>> specifics discussed here are not going to be documented in the package, but 
>> I do have to raise them to get a proper determination on what should be 
>> documented within PEPS documents.
>>
>> When you use the Da Vince-provided utility, you get a listing file 
>> complete with green-bar as if this was printed on classic 11x17 lineprinter 
>> paper, except that it is reduced and is actually on 11x8.5 paper output.  
>> Adobe reader works quite well with this format and the output of several 
>> utilities combined is auto-adjusted to make sense when you read the file.
>>
>> However, there is a nagware artifact on page one of the output that can 
>> only be removed by purchasing the package [before copying the file into 
>> PEPS!].
>>
>
> "Urge" readers/users to purchase the DaVince shareware as a CYA? 
>

Indeed.  I can only lead end users to him and in essence that is a "plug" 
for him from me.  And of course, there will be no "official" documentation 
on the nagware work-around.  I guess "somehow" some PEPS users will 
amazingly figure out just how to accomplish this (wink-wink).
 

>
>> That said, while that is the OFFICIAL way [and PEPS documentation can 
>> document this and even recommend users purchase the Da Vince package which 
>> gets a nagware removal tool or somesuch], there is an unofficial 
>> work-around "while you are waiting for your paid-for copy":
>>
>> Another part of the package is the excellent third-party freeware package 
>> PDFSAM.  [PDF Split and Merge].  This only requires installation in Windows 
>> in the usual sense.  As a minor embellishment, I don't think there is a 
>> problem with providing icons that assume one of the two releases are 
>> present for the purpose of inclusion into the main command window of PEPS 
>> [its "control center"].  The reason there are two is there is a 32-bit and 
>> a 64-bit variant.  Thus an issue here:  Must I document how to create the 
>> icon or can I just supply them both and the user deletes the one not used?  
>> Or have both of them in another directory and you move the correct one into 
>> the command center?  Does any of this even matter?  Or do I have to do none 
>> of the above and instead document tne entire process.  [Actually, I think 
>> it's not even that hard; I believe there is a check-off box to add a 
>> desktop icon, so just copy or move that into the PEPS command-center 
>> window.  This is a much smaller point, but it does have to be resolved.]
>>
>> I just re-installed PDFsam and it does have checkboxes to select whether 
> or not to create desktop icons -- it's under PDFsam BASIC's "Settings."  
> Since I just re-installed it, I probably have the PDFsam BASIC version that 
> works in both the 32-bit & 64-bit environments.
>

Yes, the newer PDFSAM is a slightly different GUI with the same overall 
results and it works fine.  I can document how I use it in a generic way; 
let users figure it out; it ain't hard to.  But it is a great tool for a 
bunch of things, such as merging a cover PDF with a body PDF into a 
combined document such as how we packaged listings of FOCAL, 1969 etc.  But 
since it can extract, it can be used to get around the Da Vince "problem" 
etc.
 

>
>  
>
>> PDFSAM is a part of the package because one thing that is done is to 
>> create PDF files out of several PDF files to be composited together having 
>> been created from different projects.
>>
>> For example, I have created a DEC-style cover page for FOCAL, 1969 that 
>> is faithful to the usual pumpkin orange rectangle and black letters you 
>> find on many DEC documents [followed by a P?S index page in standard P?S 
>> format] that is prepended to the listing file obtained likely from the Da 
>> Vince Tools, etc.  This uses the Merge capability of PDFSAM and in some 
>> cases, I actually have several document subsets knitted together.  Thus, 
>> this is a welcome tool for the PEPS package all by itself.
>>
>> Now back to the "unofficial" way to remove the nagware:
>>
>> The properly prepped LPTOUT.TXT file starts without a <FF> and ends 
>> without a <FF> but all pages within have <FF> separators.  [Again, this is 
>> handled by the STRIP.TEC macro as part of another member icon of the 
>> command center; it is actually two such icons as one is for the PTP: 
>> output.]
>>
>> Just add in a couple of lines of sacrificial junk followed by a <FF>.
>>
>> The net result is a nagwared junk page and the intended output is totally 
>> clean!
>>
>> Then use PDFSAM to split the output past page 1.  The net result is one 
>> file of the nagwared junk file, and the other is the intended output!  It's 
>> that simple.
>>
>> When you use the entire Da Vince package, there is a much larger nagware 
>> that can be ignored.  The cumbersome package is not being used, but not for 
>> the nagware reason, it is just VERY cumbersome and really not a 
>> well-thought out interface to the little command-line programs it supports 
>> [and we only need this one; there are several others].
>>
>> Moreover, there are bugs in the package that have to be worked around 
>> [which is what my customized .ini file does, no thanks to the shareware 
>> author.  It appears the package is somewhat decrepit and apparently the 
>> author gave up on updating it entirely, even though aspects of it are in 
>> varying states of dysfunctionality.  If I am correct, no changes have been 
>> made since circa 2003 or so.
>>
>
On second glance, 2005.
 

>
>> By using just the specific binary, the nagware artifact is the only way 
>> you know you are not using the registered copy. There is no add-on nagware 
>> based on using past the trial period; that is irrelevant to this usage, 
>> which is documented in the Da Vince package as to how to use it this way 
>> [which is what I did].
>>
>> If the Author has truly abandoned it, I suppose you could just add the 
> small pieces of DaVince that you are already using.  (You could still 
> recommend that DaVince be purchased as a CYA....)
>

Well, a lawyer could "urge" him to prove it is not abandoned!

There will be a list of third-party programs that are helpful in the 
poackage documentation.  this will include recommending Adobe Reader. 
 Especially Version 11 and not the newer DC crap.  Moreover I have a 
registry patch for it that gets rid of the annoying splash spleen. 
 Ironically, the same batch files for LPT2PDF have to identify where the 
ACRORDR32 program is so it can display the results afterwards.
 

>
>  
>
>> Thus, can I assume this is "just between us" and not to be brought up in 
>> the PEPS documentation even if in a euphemistic way?
>>
>> That's quite a bit of the release issues.  There are some more to be 
>> discussed in another post.
>>
>> cjl
>>
>
> Bob 
>

